@anon.158f56963078a041553d42a6ce1813d5.1087 = private unnamed_addr constant [1052 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for function definitions that use a loop variable.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AThe loop variable is not bound in the function definition, so it will always\0Ahave the value it had in the last iteration when the function is called.\0A\0AInstead, consider using a default argument to bind the loop variable at\0Afunction definition time. Or, use `functools.partial`.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Aadders = [lambda x: x + i for i in range(3)]\0Avalues = [adder(1) for adder in adders]  # [3, 3, 3]\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Aadders = [lambda x, i=i: x + i for i in range(3)]\0Avalues = [adder(1) for adder in adders]  # [1, 2, 3]\0A```\0A\0AOr:\0A```python\0Afrom functools import partial\0A\0Aadders = [partial(lambda x, i: x + i, i=i) for i in range(3)]\0Avalues = [adder(1) for adder in adders]  # [1, 2, 3]\0A```\0A\0A## References\0A- [The Hitchhiker's Guide to Python: Late Binding Closures](https://docs.python-guide.org/writing/gotchas/#late-binding-closures)\0A- [Python documentation: `functools.partial`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/functools.html#functools.partial)\0A", align 1
