@anon.158f56963078a041553d42a6ce1813d5.1161 = private unnamed_addr constant [23 x i8] c"unnecessary-placeholder", align 1
@anon.158f56963078a041553d42a6ce1813d5.1162 = private unnamed_addr constant [1283 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for unnecessary `pass` statements and ellipsis (`...`) literals in\0Afunctions, classes, and other blocks.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AIn Python, the `pass` statement and ellipsis (`...`) literal serve as\0Aplaceholders, allowing for syntactically correct empty code blocks. The\0Aprimary purpose of these nodes is to avoid syntax errors in situations\0Awhere a statement or expression is syntactically required, but no code\0Aneeds to be executed.\0A\0AIf a `pass` or ellipsis is present in a code block that includes at least\0Aone other statement (even, e.g., a docstring), it is unnecessary and should\0Abe removed.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Adef func():\0A    \22\22\22Placeholder docstring.\22\22\22\0A    pass\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Adef func():\0A    \22\22\22Placeholder docstring.\22\22\22\0A```\0A\0AOr, given:\0A```python\0Adef func():\0A    \22\22\22Placeholder docstring.\22\22\22\0A    ...\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Adef func():\0A    \22\22\22Placeholder docstring.\22\22\22\0A```\0A\0A## Fix safety\0AThis rule's fix is marked as unsafe in the rare case that the `pass` or ellipsis\0Ais followed by a string literal, since removal of the placeholder would convert the\0Asubsequent string literal into a docstring.\0A\0A## References\0A- [Python documentation: The `pass` statement](https://docs.python.org/3/reference/simple_stmts.html#the-pass-statement)\0A", align 1
