@anon.158f56963078a041553d42a6ce1813d5.1254 = private unnamed_addr constant [1167 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for bare `except` catches in `try`-`except` statements.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AA bare `except` catches `BaseException` which includes\0A`KeyboardInterrupt`, `SystemExit`, `Exception`, and others. Catching\0A`BaseException` can make it hard to interrupt the program (e.g., with\0ACtrl-C) and can disguise other problems.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Atry:\0A    raise KeyboardInterrupt(\22You probably don't mean to break CTRL-C.\22)\0Aexcept:\0A    print(\22But a bare `except` will ignore keyboard interrupts.\22)\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Atry:\0A    do_something_that_might_break()\0Aexcept MoreSpecificException as e:\0A    handle_error(e)\0A```\0A\0AIf you actually need to catch an unknown error, use `Exception` which will\0Acatch regular program errors but not important system exceptions.\0A\0A```python\0Adef run_a_function(some_other_fn):\0A    try:\0A        some_other_fn()\0A    except Exception as e:\0A        print(f\22How exceptional! {e}\22)\0A```\0A\0A## References\0A- [Python documentation: Exception hierarchy](https://docs.python.org/3/library/exceptions.html#exception-hierarchy)\0A- [Google Python Style Guide: \22Exceptions\22](https://google.github.io/styleguide/pyguide.html#24-exceptions)\0A", align 1
