@anon.158f56963078a041553d42a6ce1813d5.1307 = private unnamed_addr constant [1323 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for import statements that import a private name (a name starting\0Awith an underscore `_`) from another module.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0A[PEP 8] states that names starting with an underscore are private. Thus,\0Athey are not intended to be used outside of the module in which they are\0Adefined.\0A\0AFurther, as private imports are not considered part of the public API, they\0Aare prone to unexpected changes, especially outside of semantic versioning.\0A\0AInstead, consider using the public API of the module.\0A\0AThis rule ignores private name imports that are exclusively used in type\0Aannotations. Ideally, types would be public; however, this is not always\0Apossible when using third-party libraries.\0A\0A## Known problems\0ADoes not ignore private name imports from within the module that defines\0Athe private name if the module is defined with [PEP 420] namespace packages\0A(i.e., directories that omit the `__init__.py` file). Namespace packages\0Amust be configured via the [`namespace-packages`] setting.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Afrom foo import _bar\0A```\0A\0A## Options\0A- `namespace-packages`\0A\0A## References\0A- [PEP 8: Naming Conventions](https://peps.python.org/pep-0008/#naming-conventions)\0A- [Semantic Versioning](https://semver.org/)\0A\0A[PEP 8]: https://peps.python.org/pep-0008/\0A[PEP 420]: https://peps.python.org/pep-0420/\0A", align 1
